Hi <%= @user.login %>,

A security issue has been disclosed in OpenSSH password authentication on
Alpine Linux when OpenSSH runs without PAM. In some configurations, it
allows login without knowing a valid password. It affects an account whose
password field in /etc/shadow is exactly "*", meaning it has no usable
password. The described attack also needs another account with a stored
password hash and SSH password authentication enabled. Details:

  https://sshhole.com/

We disabled PasswordAuthentication where we found it in
/etc/ssh/sshd_config. We made this change as a precaution: your VPS may not
have had a vulnerable account. The change does not mean we found a
compromise.

The change applies to these VPSes:

<% @changed_vpses.each do |vps| -%>
  - VPS <%= vps.id %> <%= vps.hostname %>
<% end -%>

The original configuration is saved on each of those VPSes at:

  /etc/ssh/sshd_config.before-2026-11-05-disable-password-authentication

Restoring the original configuration could enable password login again.

We recommend updating your system, including OpenSSH, and
restarting sshd so it runs the updated version. Check your login and system
logs, especially for unfamiliar successful logins, for signs of a possible
earlier compromise.

To log in with a key, you can upload your public SSH key to vpsAdmin and
then deploy it from the details of the running VPS. Saving the key in your
profile alone does not install it in an existing VPS. Instructions:

  https://kb.vpsfree.org/manuals/server/ssh

You can also log in through the remote console in vpsAdmin:

  https://kb.vpsfree.org/manuals/vps/console

Best regards,

vpsFree.cz team
